{
  "Comment": "Table-builder control plane (SPEC-070, extended by SPEC-143). TWO branches over ONE spine: validate-authorize → {check-table-absent | load-table-definition} → inspect-source → infer-schema → {execute-ddl → RecordSuccess → Succeeded | the SPEC-144 refresh tail}. inspect-source and infer-schema are SHARED by both and know nothing about mode — load-table-definition normalises the state into exactly what a create would have handed them (rewritten prefix, reconstructed declared partition keys), which is why a refresh needed a second entry verb rather than a flag. Each Task threads the running request object via Payload.$=$ + OutputPath=$.Payload. Transient errors are retried on every verb: Lambda service faults, plus each verb's own throttleable AWS call mapped in-Lambda to the TransientError these Retry lists name — validate-authorize's registry read, check-table-absent's Glue GetTable, inspect-source's S3 listing, infer-schema's S3 head probe, execute-ddl's Athena polls. Any terminal verb failure is caught and routed to the shared recorded-failure state, which shapes the structured outcome { requestId, actor, database, tableName, status:'failed', reason } SPEC-072 notifies from; RecordSuccess shapes the success twin, whose `status` is THREADED from the verb that produced it ('created' | 'unchanged' | 'refreshed'). A partitioned table needs NO MSCK REPAIR (projection, SPEC-069) — the single execute-ddl CREATE is terminal for both flat and partitioned sources.",
  "StartAt": "ValidateAuthorize",
  "States": {
    "ValidateAuthorize": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "First step. The Pipe forwards the raw SQS batch (a JSON ARRAY), so Payload.$=$ hands the array to the validator (it reads record[0]); OutputPath=$.Payload makes the validated request object the whole state for the next steps. A failure here runs BEFORE the request identity exists, so its Catch (no ResultPath — the input is an array, not an object) routes to ValidateFailureSeed, which seeds null identity fields so the shared recorded-failure state can reference them uniformly.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${validate_authorize_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "Next": "ValidateFailureSeed"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RouteByMode"
    },
    "RouteByMode": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "SPEC-143. A refresh is a re-run of inspect-source + infer-schema against a table that already EXISTS, so it takes the mirror image of check-table-absent: load-table-definition, which reads the live Glue table, decides whether it is refreshable, and rewrites space.prefix to the table's own location so the two shared verbs need no mode branch. THE IsPresent CONJUNCT IS NOT DECORATION: a Choice rule whose Variable path is absent raises States.Runtime, which no Catch anywhere can catch, so a bare StringEquals would KILL an execution rather than fall through to Default on the very input Default exists for — an envelope written before this spec, or one in flight while the two deploy workflows land out of order.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "StringEquals": "refresh" }
          ],
          "Next": "LoadTableDefinition"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "CheckTableAbsent"
    },
    "LoadTableDefinition": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${load_table_definition_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InspectSource"
    },
    "CheckTableAbsent": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${check_table_absent_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InspectSource"
    },
    "InspectSource": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${inspect_source_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "InferSchema"
    },
    "InferSchema": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${infer_schema_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RouteTailByMode"
    },
    "RouteTailByMode": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "SPEC-143. The two branches share everything up to here and diverge on what they do with the inferred schema: a create executes its CREATE, a refresh must DIFF the new columns against the ones the table already declares and ALTER. That tail is SPEC-144's diff-schema, which classifies the change and enforces the removal opt-in; the apply behind it is still SPEC-143's not_implemented stub until SPEC-144 Phase 3 replaces it.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.mode", "StringEquals": "refresh" }
          ],
          "Next": "DiffSchema"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "ExecuteDdl"
    },
    "DiffSchema": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "SPEC-144. Classifies the change into added/removed/retyped/unchanged and enforces the removal opt-in. A removal without allowRemovals is a TERMINAL failure that changes nothing and names the columns in its notification — the refusal IS the warning. This verb also emits the no-change notification itself: the machine has no notification state, and on the unchanged path no Lambda runs after this one.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${diff_schema_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RouteByChange"
    },
    "RouteByChange": {
      "Type": "Choice",
      "Comment": "An unchanged schema succeeds WITHOUT issuing any DDL — no drop, no create. BOTH outcomes are explicit rules and the Default is a FAILURE, which is the opposite of the mode Choices and deliberately so: those default to the create path because an absent mode has an obvious correct reading, whereas an absent $.diff.changed means diff-schema returned something this machine cannot interpret. Defaulting that to RecordSuccess would hand it to a Pass state that reads $.status — raising the same uncatchable States.Runtime one state later, with the execution stranded and NO recorded outcome. So it records one instead.",
      "Choices": [
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "BooleanEquals": true }
          ],
          "Next": "RefreshNotImplemented"
        },
        {
          "And": [
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "IsPresent": true },
            { "Variable": "$.diff.changed", "BooleanEquals": false }
          ],
          "Next": "RecordSuccess"
        }
      ],
      "Default": "DiffMissingSeed"
    },
    "DiffMissingSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Unreachable while diff-schema always emits $.diff — which is the only reason a seed is cheap enough to be worth having. Same shape as a Task Catch's output so RecordFailure needs no special case.",
      "Result": {
        "Error": "TerminalError",
        "Cause": "{\"errorType\":\"TerminalError\",\"errorMessage\":\"unexpected_error: diff-schema returned no diff\"}"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.error",
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "RefreshNotImplemented": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "SPEC-143 FR-008 — REPLACED WHOLESALE BY SPEC-144. Shaped exactly like a Task Catch's output (a {Error, Cause} object merged onto $.error via ResultPath) so RecordFailure needs no special case and cpcommon.errors.failure_reason decodes 'not_implemented' from the Cause the same way it decodes a real verb's. A Fail state would have been the obvious spelling and is the wrong one: it ends the execution immediately, so the structured { status:'failed', reason } outcome would never be recorded. THE EQUIVALENCE IS TO THE RECORD, NOT TO THE NOTIFICATION: every user-facing message on this plane is emitted from inside a verb (notifies_on_failure), and a Pass runs no Lambda, so a refresh stopping here tells the user nothing. That is acceptable only because nothing but a hand-crafted SQS message can reach it before SPEC-144 replaces the state with a real verb.",
      "Result": {
        "Error": "TerminalError",
        "Cause": "{\"errorType\":\"TerminalError\",\"errorMessage\":\"not_implemented: the refresh diff and apply land in SPEC-144\"}"
      },
      "ResultPath": "$.error",
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "ExecuteDdl": {
      "Type": "Task",
      "Comment": "Run the single CREATE via Athena (projection ⇒ no MSCK REPAIR). A transient Athena/S3 error is raised as TransientError and retried here (NOT terminal); a FAILED/CANCELLED query or timeout raises TerminalError → Catch → recorded failure.",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:states:::lambda:invoke",
      "Parameters": {
        "FunctionName": "${execute_ddl_function_arn}",
        "Payload.$": "$"
      },
      "OutputPath": "$.Payload",
      "Retry": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": [
            "Lambda.ServiceException",
            "Lambda.TooManyRequestsException",
            "Lambda.AWSLambdaException",
            "Lambda.SdkClientException",
            "TransientError"
          ],
          "IntervalSeconds": 2,
          "MaxAttempts": 3,
          "BackoffRate": 2
        }
      ],
      "Catch": [
        {
          "ErrorEquals": ["States.ALL"],
          "ResultPath": "$.error",
          "Next": "RecordFailure"
        }
      ],
      "Next": "RecordSuccess"
    },
    "RecordSuccess": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "Shape the terminal SUCCESS outcome SPEC-072 notifies from. `status` is now THREADED rather than hard-coded (SPEC-144): execute-ddl sets 'created', diff-schema sets 'unchanged' on the no-op path, apply-refresh sets 'refreshed'. Every state that can reach here sets it — which it must, because a Pass state's JSONPath raises an UNCATCHABLE States.Runtime on an absent key, and it would raise it AFTER the work succeeded.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "database.$": "$.space.database",
        "tableName.$": "$.tableName",
        "mode.$": "$.mode",
        "status.$": "$.status"
      },
      "Next": "Succeeded"
    },
    "ValidateFailureSeed": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "A validate-authorize failure occurs before the request identity exists (its input was the raw SQS array), so its Catch replaced the state with the error object. Seed null identity fields + carry the error under $.error so the shared RecordFailure path is uniform for every verb.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId": null,
        "actor": null,
        "space": { "database": null },
        "tableName": null,
        "error.$": "$"
      },
      "Next": "RecordFailure"
    },
    "RecordFailure": {
      "Type": "Pass",
      "Comment": "The single recorded-failure state every verb's Catch reaches. Shapes the structured outcome { requestId, actor, database, tableName, status:'failed', error } (identity is null for a pre-identity validate failure) then fails the execution. It carries the RAW $.error {Error, Cause}, not a decoded reason: the ParseError Pass state that used to produce one ran States.StringToJson, which raises an UNCATCHABLE States.Runtime whenever Cause is absent or is not JSON (States.Timeout, States.DataLimitExceeded, Lambda.Unknown — verified live), killing the execution before it could record anything. Decode with cpcommon.errors.failure_reason, never in ASL.",
      "Parameters": {
        "requestId.$": "$.requestId",
        "actor.$": "$.actor",
        "database.$": "$.space.database",
        "tableName.$": "$.tableName",
        "status": "failed",
        "error.$": "$.error"
      },
      "Next": "Failed"
    },
    "Succeeded": {
      "Type": "Succeed"
    },
    "Failed": {
      "Type": "Fail",
      "Error": "TableBuildFailed",
      "Cause": "The table-builder control plane recorded an unrecoverable error (see the RecordFailure state output for the structured { status:'failed', reason } outcome)."
    }
  }
}
